 ## Overview
-This method segments 3D Gaussian primitives into distinct semantic instances by training per-Gaussian features using contrastive learning on 2D masks [S1]. It solves the problem of object-level segmentation in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scenes, where primitives do not strictly model geometry and may be placed around surfaces or shared between objects [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [DeSplat](12925-desplat.md) by relying on feature similarity in a learned contrastive space rather than geometric clustering or explicit boundary detection, enabling interactive segmentation via single-click queries [S1].
+This method segments 3D Gaussian primitives into distinct object instances by training per-Gaussian semantic features using contrastive learning on 2D masks derived from text queries [S1]. It solves the problem of assigning semantic identity to Gaussian primitives, which do not strictly model object geometry and often include low-opacity primitives or large primitives shared among objects [S1]. It differs from sibling methods by relying on interactive 2D mask generation via GroundingDINO and SAM-HQ, followed by contrastive feature optimization and space-carving post-processing, rather than language-guided training or generalizable encoders [S1, 12438-langsplat-training-procedure.md, 16774-generalizable-semantic-gaussian-splatting.md].
 
 ## How it works
-The method consists of three phases: 2D mask generation, contrastive feature training, and interactive segmentation with post-processing.
